{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012R65XRQzYvFaa8wUXJWgSY","type":"tool_result","content":"499\tSJ $\\rho$ decreases with entity count (0.45 $\\to$ 0.34) as combinatorial complexity increases, and peaks at medium sentence length (0.44), consistent with the inverted-U pattern observed in the F1-bucket analysis.\n500\t\n501\t\\subsection{Out-of-Domain Robustness}\n502\t\\label{sec:ood}\n503\t\n504\t\\begin{table}[t]\n505\t\\centering\n506\t\\small\n507\t\\caption{In-domain vs.\\ OOD comparison (WNUT$\\to$CoNLL).}\n508\t\\label{tab:ood}\n509\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n510\t\\toprule\n511\t\\textbf{Setting} & \\textbf{F1} & \\textbf{SJ $\\rho$} & \\textbf{FK $\\rho$} & \\textbf{LP $\\rho$} & \\textbf{SJ$-$LP} \\\\\n512\t\\midrule\n513\tIn-domain & 0.541 & 0.37 & 0.28 & 0.22 & +0.15 \\\\\n514\tOOD & 0.420 & 0.33 & 0.24 & 0.21 & +0.12 \\\\\n515\t\\bottomrule\n516\t\\end{tabular}\n517\t\\end{table}\n518\t\n519\tUnder domain shift (WNUT-trained model evaluated on CoNLL, Table~\\ref{tab:ood}), SJ remains the strongest signal ($\\rho{=}0.33$, $\\Delta{=}+0.12$ over log-prob). SJ-based best-of-$N$ selection achieves F1$=$0.455, a +3.5pp improvement over greedy (0.42). In OOD settings, log-prob is particularly unreliable due to model miscalibration, while SJ's behavior-based measurement---directly observing output consistency---degrades more gracefully.\n520\t\n521\t%==============================================================================\n522\t\\section{Discussion}\n523\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n524\t%==============================================================================\n525\t\n526\t\\paragraph{Why structural consistency outperforms model confidence.}\n527\tThe $\\Delta\\rho \\geq 0.12$ gap between SJ and log-prob across all NER settings reveals a fundamental distinction. Log-probability is a \\emph{sequence-level} signal that includes contributions from all tokens---format markers, type labels, punctuation---most of which are near-deterministic and uninformative about entity correctness. A sequence may have high log-prob because the format is standard, yet contain an incorrect entity span. SJ operates at the \\emph{set level}, directly comparing entity-level outputs and capturing information orthogonal to model confidence.\n528\t\n529\t\\paragraph{The compound error boundary.}\n530\tOur diagnostic framework identifies a principled boundary for consistency-based TTS: when the task requires simultaneous correctness of multiple components (as in relation extraction, where head span, tail span, and relation type must all be correct), compound error propagation degrades all confidence signals. This is not a limitation of SJ specifically---it affects log-prob, FK, and entity voting equally. The conditional analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:threshold}) shows that within the model's capability range, SJ remains effective even for RE ($\\rho{=}0.39$--$0.44$).\n531\t\n532\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n533\tFor practitioners applying TTS to NER: (1) SJ is the recommended selection signal; (2) the additional computation cost is negligible ($O(N^2)$ set comparisons vs.\\ $O(N)$ LLM forward passes); (3) the SJ--logprob gap widens with $N$, meaning SJ's advantage grows with compute budget; (4) SJ is robust to temperature choice, requiring no hyperparameter tuning. For RE, TTS benefits are limited unless the model has demonstrated NER competence on the input.\n534\t\n535\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n536\tOur study is limited to 8B-parameter models; larger models may exhibit different uncertainty patterns. We evaluate on English-language datasets only. The soft Jaccard metric requires parsed structured outputs, adding a dependency on output format compliance. We do not compare against trained verifiers or reward models, which could potentially outperform unsupervised signals at additional training cost.\n537\t\n538\t%==============================================================================\n539\t\\section{Conclusion}\n540\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n541\t%==============================================================================\n542\t\n543\tWe presented a systematic comparison of confidence signals for information extraction test-time scaling. Set-aware self-consistency (soft Jaccard) consistently outperforms sequence log-probability, Fleiss' $\\kappa$, exact-match voting, and entity-level majority voting for NER, with $\\Delta\\rho \\geq 0.12$ over log-prob and +2.2--2.5pp F1 via best-of-$N$ selection. For relation extraction, we identified compound error propagation as a universal bottleneck that degrades all confidence signals, establishing a task-complexity boundary for consistency-based methods. These findings provide actionable guidance for practitioners deploying LLM-based IE systems: when sampling multiple candidates, use set-aware structural comparison for selection, and expect diminishing returns as task complexity increases beyond entity-level extraction.\n544\t\n545\t%==============================================================================\n546\t% References\n547\t%==============================================================================\n548\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n549\t\n550\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2024)]{chen2024universal}\n551\tXinyun Chen, Renat Aksitov, Uri Alon, Jie Ren, Kefan Xiao, Pengcheng Yin, Sushant Prakash, Charles Sutton, Xuezhi Wang, and Denny Zhou. 2024.\n552\t\\newblock Universal self-consistency for large language model generation.\n553\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NAACL}, pages 1--15.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[Cobbe et~al.(2021)]{cobbe2021training}\n556\tKarl Cobbe, Vineet Kosaraju, Mohammad Bavarian, Mark Chen, Heewoo Jun, Lukasz Kaiser, Matthias Plappert, Jerry Tworek, Jacob Hilton, Reiichiro Nakano, et~al. 2021.\n557\t\\newblock Training verifiers to solve math word problems.\n558\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2110.14168}.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[Derczynski et~al.(2017)]{derczynski2017results}\n561\tLeon Derczynski, Eric Nichols, Marieke van Erp, and Nut Limsopatham. 2017.\n562\t\\newblock Results of the {WNUT}2017 shared task on novel and emerging entity recognition.\n563\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of the 3rd Workshop on Noisy User-generated Text}, pages 140--147.\n564\t\n565\t\\bibitem[Derczynski et~al.(2016)]{derczynski2016broad}\n566\tLeon Derczynski, Diana Maynard, Giuseppe Rizzo, Marieke van Erp, Genevieve Gorrell, Rapha{\\\"e}l Troncy, Johann Petrak, and Kalina Bontcheva. 2016.\n567\t\\newblock Analysis of named entity recognition and linking for tweets.\n568\t\\newblock \\emph{Information Processing \\& Management}, 51(2):32--49.\n569\t\n570\t\\bibitem[Desai and Durrett(2020)]{desai2020calibration}\n571\tShrey Desai and Greg Durrett. 2020.\n572\t\\newblock Calibration of pre-trained transformers.\n573\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, pages 295--312.\n574\t\n575\t\\bibitem[El-Yaniv and Wiener(2010)]{el2010foundations}\n576\tRan El-Yaniv and Yair Wiener. 2010.\n577\t\\newblock On the foundations of noise-free selective classification.\n578\t\\newblock \\emph{Journal of Machine Learning Research}, 11:1605--1641.\n579\t\n580\t\\bibitem[Fleiss(1971)]{fleiss1971measuring}\n581\tJoseph~L. Fleiss. 1971.\n582\t\\newblock Measuring nominal scale agreement among many raters.\n583\t\\newblock \\emph{Psychological Bulletin}, 76(5):378.\n584\t\n585\t\\bibitem[Geifman and El-Yaniv(2017)]{geifman2017selective}\n586\tYonatan Geifman and Ran El-Yaniv. 2017.\n587\t\\newblock Selective classification for deep neural networks.\n588\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}, pages 4878--4887.\n589\t\n590\t\\bibitem[Hu et~al.(2022)]{hu2022lora}\n591\tEdward~J. Hu, Yelong Shen, Phillip Wallis, Zeyuan Allen-Zhu, Yuanzhi Li, Shean Wang, Lu Wang, and Weizhu Chen. 2022.\n592\t\\newblock {LoRA}: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\n593\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n594\t\n595\t\\bibitem[Jiang et~al.(2021)]{jiang2021can}\n596\tZhengbao Jiang, Jun Araki, Haibo Ding, and Graham Neubig. 2021.\n597\t\\newblock How can we know when language models know? {O}n the calibration of language models for question answering.\n598\t\\newblock \\emph{Transactions of the ACL}, 9:962--977.\n599\t\n600\t\\bibitem[Kadavath et~al.(2022)]{kadavath2022language}\n601\tSaurav Kadavath, Tom Conerly, Amanda Askell, Tom Henighan, Dawn Drain, Ethan Perez, Nicholas Schiefer, Zac Hatfield-Dodds, Nova DasSarma, Eli Tyre, et~al. 2022.\n602\t\\newblock Language models (mostly) know what they know.\n603\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2207.05221}.\n604\t\n605\t\\bibitem[Kuhn et~al.(2023)]{kuhn2023semantic}\n606\tLorenz Kuhn, Yarin Gal, and Sebastian Farquhar. 2023.\n607\t\\newblock Semantic uncertainty: Linguistic invariances for uncertainty estimation in natural language generation.\n608\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICML}, pages 17960--17885.\n609\t\n610\t\\bibitem[Kwon et~al.(2023)]{kwon2023efficient}\n611\tWoosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li, Siyuan Zhuang, Ying Sheng, Lianmin Zheng, Cody~Hao Yu, Joseph~E. Gonzalez, Hao Zhang, and Ion Stoica. 2023.\n612\t\\newblock Efficient memory management for large language model serving with {PagedAttention}.\n613\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of SOSP}.\n614\t\n615\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2023)]{li2023evaluating}\n616\tJunlong Li, Zhuosheng Zhang, and Hai Zhao. 2023.\n617\t\\newblock Evaluating {ChatGPT}'s information extraction capabilities: An assessment of performance, explainability, calibration, and faithfulness.\n618\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.11633}.\n619\t\n620\t\\bibitem[Lightman et~al.(2024)]{lightman2024lets}\n621\tHunter Lightman, Vineet Kosaraju, Yura Burda, Harri Edwards, Bowen Baker, Teddy Lee, Jan Leike, John Schulman, Ilya Sutskever, and Karl Cobbe. 2024.\n622\t\\newblock Let's verify step by step.\n623\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n624\t\n625\t\\bibitem[Luan et~al.(2018)]{luan2018multi}\n626\tYi Luan, Luheng He, Mari Ostendorf, and Hannaneh Hajishirzi. 2018.\n627\t\\newblock Multi-task identification of entities, relations, and coreference for scientific knowledge graph construction.\n628\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, pages 3219--3232.\n629\t\n630\t\\bibitem[Madaan et~al.(2023)]{madaan2023self}\n631\tAman Madaan, Niket Tandon, Prakhar Gupta, Skyler Hallinan, Luyu Gao, Sarah Wiegreffe, Uri Alon, Nouha Dziri, Shrimai Prabhumoye, Yiming Yang, et~al. 2023.\n632\t\\newblock Self-refine: Iterative refinement with self-feedback.\n633\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}.\n634\t\n635\t\\bibitem[Manakul et~al.(2023)]{manakul2023selfcheckgpt}\n636\tPotsawee Manakul, Adian Liusie, and Mark~J.F. Gales. 2023.\n637\t\\newblock {SelfCheckGPT}: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models.\n638\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, pages 9004--9017.\n639\t\n640\t\\bibitem[Qwen Team(2025)]{qwen2025qwen3}\n641\tQwen Team. 2025.\n642\t\\newblock Qwen3 technical report.\n643\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388}.\n644\t\n645\t\\bibitem[Tjong Kim~Sang and De~Meulder(2003)]{tjong2003introduction}\n646\tErik~F. Tjong Kim~Sang and Fien De~Meulder. 2003.\n647\t\\newblock Introduction to the {CoNLL}-2003 shared task: Language-independent named entity recognition.\n648\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of CoNLL}, pages 142--147.\n649\t\n650\t\\bibitem[Touvron et~al.(2023)]{touvron2023llama}\n651\tHugo Touvron, Louis Martin, Kevin Stone, Peter Albert, et~al. 2023.\n652\t\\newblock {LLaMA} 2: Open foundation and fine-tuned chat models.\n653\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.09288}.\n654\t\n655\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2023a)]{wang2023selfconsistency}\n656\tXuezhi Wang, Jason Wei, Dale Schuurmans, Quoc Le, Ed~Chi, Sharan Narang, Aakanksha Chowdhery, and Denny Zhou. 2023a.\n657\t\\newblock Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models.\n658\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of ICLR}.\n659\t\n660\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2023b)]{wang2023instructuie}\n661\tXiao Wang, Weikang Zhou, Can Zu, Han Xia, Tianze Chen, Yuansen Zhang, Rui Zheng, Junjie Ye, Qi Zhang, Tao Gui, Jihua Zhu, Jingsheng Yang, Siyuan Li, and Chunsai Du. 2023b.\n662\t\\newblock {InstructUIE}: Multi-task instruction tuning for unified information extraction.\n663\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.08085}.\n664\t\n665\t\\bibitem[Xu et~al.(2024)]{xu2024large}\n666\tDerong Xu, Wei Chen, Wenjun Peng, Chao Zhang, Tong Xu, Xiangyu Zhao, Xian Wu, Yefeng Zheng, and Enhong Chen. 2024.\n667\t\\newblock Large language models for generative information extraction: A survey.\n668\t\\newblock \\emph{Frontiers of Computer Science}, 18(6):186357.\n669\t\n670\t\\bibitem[Yao et~al.(2023)]{yao2023tree}\n671\tShunyu Yao, Dian Yu, Jeffrey Zhao, Izhak Shafran, Thomas~L. Griffiths, Yuan Cao, and Karthik Narasimhan. 2023.\n672\t\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n673\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of NeurIPS}.\n674\t\n675\t\\end{thebibliography}\n676\t\n677\t\\end{document}\n678\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPW2YJdz9hnSyphukG2PoF","session_id":"bee9bcb9-e915-4303-a88d-b9f96b0f305a","uuid":"b719e0c2-9538-4be9-a3a7-4af5cff57e7e","timestamp":"2026-05-14T11:54:01.015Z"}
